{
  // The scanner's NeoPixel ring as a device: 35 pixels in a circle, on the
  // stage the scanner looks render into.
  //
  // Coordinates are literal, in the same space as the obelisk device (x is
  // across, y is up, the obelisk's floor at y=0 and its top at y=42): the
  // ring is a circle of radius 3.5 centred at (3.5, 14.0), a third of the way
  // up the sculpture and spanning its width - the way the scanner is mounted
  // on the tower. That is the point of it: a look that travels along y
  // starts at the foot of the obelisk (where the DMX truss stands, in
  // config/scanner_stage.json), passes through the ring and climbs on to the
  // top. Pixel 0 sits at the top of the circle, numbered the way the physical
  // ring runs; the centre and radius are kRing* in
  // src/relics/scanner/scanner_patterns.h, and the live game writes the same
  // circle inline - main-py/lib/jr_lib/eclipse_engine.py - so the three have
  // to agree (python/tests checks that they do).
  //
  // The viewer draws its picture from these same fields, which is why they
  // are a real circle and not a convenient line.
  //
  // Preview output: this device's frames only ever go to a screen - the real
  // ring is painted by the game (or, at a desk over ssh, by the pi's ring
  // bridge) out of the frame stream, never by a wire.

  "name": "scanner_ring",

  "coord_space": "literal",

  "output": {
    "type": "preview",
    "fps": 30
  },

